2
Interactive
Someone logs on at the computer's own keyboard and screen.
An employee signs in to their office PC in the morning.
A local keyboard logon on a locked server room machine at 3 AM, or by an account that never uses that machine.
3
Network
Access to a shared resource over the network, such as a file share. No desktop is created.
A user opens a shared folder on the file server.
One workstation making type 3 logons to many hosts in minutes (lateral movement), or many type 3 failures against an admin account.
4
Batch
A scheduled task or batch job runs under an account.
A nightly backup task runs.
A new scheduled task running under a high-privilege account, started from an unknown script.
5
Service
A Windows service starts under its configured account.
A known service starts at boot.
A newly installed unknown service logging on as an admin account (see 7045 and 4697).
7
Unlock
A locked workstation is unlocked.
A user returns from lunch and unlocks their PC.
An unlock at a time the user was away or on leave.
10
RemoteInteractive
Logon through Remote Desktop (RDP) or similar remote sessions.
An IT administrator connects to a server from the office network.
An RDP logon from an unknown or foreign IP address, or many RDP failures followed by a success.
11
CachedInteractive
Logon using cached credentials, when the domain controller is not reachable.
A laptop user signs in while travelling with no network.
A cached logon on a desktop that should always be connected, or logons by an account that has been disabled in the domain.


